That's the decisive code, and it does change things:

```cpp
if (container->enterSerial == serial) return true;
for (auto it = container->serials.begin(); ...) if (*it == serial) return true;
```

A serial stays valid — the enter serial persists, and past serials sit in a ring buffer. So mako *can* cache a serial and reuse it later. Checking what fills that ring, because it decides whether this is reliable:

